A||||16||GT Bastard||AUTO OR MANUAL??? I CAN'T DECIDE!!!!!!!||||4||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||03-19-2000||01:52 PM||||Right now I'm torn between having a local fiero-nut here make my auto GT into a manual transmission car(he's done several other swaps and seems to really know his sh!t) or just putting the money into other mods like headers, exhaust, porting, and so on....  The whole job will probably cost me around 1000 bucks, which isn't too bad. Is a manual THAT fun? ||||reg||4
Z||000001||88formula||03-19-2000||03:56 PM||||A manual is more fun than an automatic, but a manual WILL give you more problems than an automatic will (i.e. clutch, hydraulics, and those cables su_k). If you could find a donor car or one in the junkyard you could do it yourself for much less than a grand.  I don't think a manual swap is worth 1G and I think any monkey could do this himself or herself unless they didn’t want to go through the trouble.My advice is don't do it that way.<p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 03-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||FIEROCIOUS||03-19-2000||04:31 PM||||More fun raceing, (nesasary) Less fun on the street!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Shiner||03-19-2000||05:03 PM||||My first Fiero had a slushbox in it.  My GT has a getrag, and I will NEVER own another automatic car so long as I live!  I literally don't know what to do with myself when I am driving an automatic.  Get a manual trans!  All the trouble really is worth it, and you really don't get that feeling like you are "one" with the car in an auto, IMHO.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Patrick||03-19-2000||05:08 PM||||<BR>A Fiero with an automatic is strictly for a Fiero enthusiast, whereas a Fiero with a standard is for not only a Fiero enthusiast, but also for a <I>driving</I> enthusiast.<P>Your choice ultimately depends on what category you place yourself in.||||reg||14
Z||000005||Eric||03-19-2000||05:41 PM||||Jeez, everybody calm down.  GT Bastard, have you ever driven a manual trans Fiero?  You may decide you don't even like it.  The clutch is really heavy and there's always the hydraulic problems.  Unless you can find a low milage one, I would suggest getting it serviced which is going to be some major$.  $1000 isn't all that steep when you figure in routing the clutch line, installing the shifter, and all that other mumbo jumbo associated with manual transmissions.  To do the job halfassed you'd have atleast $1500 in it.  And to do it right, who knows?  If it was me, I'd look into buying another car with the manual already installed than trying this.  Besides, $1000 put in the right places can make a very fast car.  You can even get a shift kit for the auto.||||reg||1
Z||000006||SCCA FIERO||03-19-2000||06:42 PM||||My friend and I installed a 5 speed Isuzu transmission in a Fiero. It cost $150 for the tranny, shifter, cables and the slave from a boneyard 86 4 cylinder car. He bought a new master cylinder and enough line to plumb it and had some extra pedals laying around. It was simple to do. The shifter just bolts down where the auto selector bolts down, the master has a place for it to go, and the lines weren't too hard to run. The 4 speed tranny is the hard ones to shift... heavy pedal and very notchy. The Isuzu tranny shifts like silk and the pedal will fall to the floor if you look at it hard enough. The Getrag would be a nice choice, but those things are hard to find and are expensive. I think it can be done for less than half, no, I KNOW it can be done for less than half of $1000. <BR><p>[This message has been edited by SCCA FIERO (edited 03-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||GT Bastard||03-19-2000||07:45 PM||||I used to have an '84 4sp that was a little notchy, but I still enjoyed it.  I'm not sure if I'm technically prepared to do something like that, but I'd like to do it myself.  SCCA, did you run into any other problems?  Does the computer need be swapped from another V6 manual car? Please keep the responses flowing guys!  thanks...||||reg||1
Z||000008||SCCA FIERO||03-19-2000||08:58 PM||||As you may know I have a V6 in my 88 Coupe with the Isuzu tranny. The engine came out of an 86SE 4 speed as did the computer, the same chip is in the computer. The only problem with it is the computer doesn't know there is a 5th gear, so the shift light doesn't come on to tell me to shift to 5th.<P>The car we put the manual tranny in was an 85 4 cylinder car with an 87 motor.<P>We also put an Isuzu in an 86GT in place of the 4 speed and used the same computer. We did have to switch shifters because the 4 speed shifter would not go into 5th and reverse.<p>[This message has been edited by SCCA FIERO (edited 03-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||03-19-2000||10:14 PM||||Don't forget when you switch from stick the auto, or the other way, that you need to chip the ECM.<P>If you live anyplace with traffic stay with the auto. I've driven both types of cars for years. Stick stinks in traffic.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Oreif||03-19-2000||11:10 PM||||First, Let me say that mine is an automatic, my first one was a 4-spd and I've driven 5-spds. Yes a manual is fun but it is a pain when things go wrong and sometimes very expensive.  But my automatic with a shift kit and the engine modified to 170hp it's still fun to drive! Remeber theres more to being a "driving Enthusiast" then shifting!<P>Second if you are going to swap from an auto to a manual there are some things that you need to consider. Like how to start the car? The neutral safety switch that lets the car start in park or neutral has to be modified so you can hook it to the clutch pedal and be able to start the car only when clutch is depressed. Does your car have cruise control? If it does the manual cars have it set up so that the clutch OR the brake disengage it. You wouldn't want to be cruising down the highway then put the clutch in just to watch the engine scream to redline would you? The other thing is reverse lights you will need to splice in the switch for the stick. Finally most automatic Fiero owners never use the parking brake (I do!) so the cables could be frozen. You will need to replace these to be able to park it. The cables are about $200 for the entire set (3 of them). The actual swap isn't bad it's the little things that'll get you.||||reg||4
Z||000011||Patrick||03-20-2000||04:28 AM||||<BR>Yeah, there's more to being a "driving enthusiast" than shifting, but not <I>much</I> more... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Instead of converting an automatic to a standard, I think it's a heck of a lot easier just to buy a vehicle that originally came from the factory equipped that way. But, if you're up to the challenge...||||reg||14
Z||000012||Eric||03-20-2000||11:21 AM||||Maybe it won't cost as much as I previously thought if you do it yourself.  But I don't think you should even consider letting someone at a shop who you have to pay buy the hour do this swap for you.  In both cases your Fiero is going to be down for quite a while.  I've had so many problems and continue to have problems with my getrag and clutch hydraulics, that sometimes I seriously consider swaping in an auto.  Maybe the Isuzu is a better bet.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Eric||03-20-2000||11:25 AM||||And the car will be worth $0 more after you're done with this swap.  If you put a grand in the paint or the engine, you would be able to expect to recover atleast some of your losses.  The autos may even be worth more now because there are less problems associated with them.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Oreif||03-20-2000||12:24 PM||||Also I found out you will need to replace the axles they are different lengths between autos and manuals.||||reg||4
Z||000015||coyote||03-20-2000||02:09 PM||||There sure seem to be alot of opinions here so let me give you mine. I have personally converted 2 cars in the last 2 weeks From auto's to 5 speeds. While this may seem easy at first, i asure you it is a fairly large undertaking. First you need the trans, weather it is an izusu or getrag is of no consequence other then the shift cables being diiferent. costs are typicaly $500 - $1000 for the parts. One thing to look for is a steel clutch pedal. Neutral safety, cruise control and reverse lights are also things you would need to consider. Just make sure the donor car matches yours and grab the harness as well.one other thing to look out for is that there are 2 different set- ups for pedals and hydraulic pumps, 1 has the bushing on the pedal the other on the push rod of the pump, so no mix and matching. For a preferance i would go for the getrag it has better gearing then the isuzu. I prefer the stick to the automatic , unless traffic is a consideration. You need to decide, what does your style of driving lend itself to??.  Good luck<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000016||Monkeyman||03-20-2000||09:50 PM||||88formula--No.  I could not do this myself.  (Not that I would want to.)||||reg||1
